  "answer": "Mitosis and meiosis are both types of cell division, but they have key differences. Mitosis is the process by which a cell divides into two identical daughter cells, and it occurs in somatic (body) cells. Meiosis, on the other hand, is a specialized type of cell division that occurs in the reproductive organs to produce gametes (sperm and eggs). Mitosis results in two genetically identical cells, while meiosis results in four genetically diverse cells. Additionally, meiosis involves two stages of cell division, whereas mitosis involves only one stage. Meiosis also reduces the number of chromosomes by half, which is essential for sexual reproduction.",
